Example — `sibil preflight --connect SIB-CONNECT-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX`
================================================================

This is the exact, only outbound request the ephemeral preflight probe sends.
It is generated from `cmd/preflight.go::sendPreflightProbe`. No service names,
process list, ports, logs, or file paths are ever included.

POST /api/v1/preflight/probe HTTP/1.1
Host: monitor.cordee.ovh
Content-Type: application/json
Content-Length: 162

{
  "connect_code": "SIB-CONNECT-9F2K-3LMQ-7XPZ",
  "os": "linux",
  "arch": "amd64",
  "can_run": true,
  "tunnel_reachable": true,
  "pm2_detected": true,
  "systemd_detected": true,
  "docker_detected": "not_detected",
  "persistent_install": false
}

Response (200):

{
  "status": "ok",
  "compatible": true
}

What this payload can never contain (enforced by the code, not by policy):
  - process names or PM2 app list
  - listening ports
  - log lines or log file paths
  - filesystem paths
  - environment variables
  - infrastructure inventory of any kind

`sibil preflight` without `--connect` sends nothing at all — it only opens a
plain GET to `/health` to check that the relay is reachable, and prints a
local report. No code path persists this probe to disk.
